UID:10022870-1793725200-1793730000@events.ubc.ca
SUMMARY:Pedagogies of Play
DESCRIPTION:This first workshop in The Critical Play Speakers’ Series focuses on how the interactive\, dynamic experiences at the heart of games can be used as new kind of teaching experience. Christopher Kawika Guillermo (Dr Christopher B Patterson) of UBC’s Institute for Gender\, Race\, Sexuality and Social Justice\, and Vancouver-based game designer and programmer Tim Bellefleur from Chronograph Games\, reveal their pedagogies of play\, and how the unexpected\, emergent properties of games create new opportunities for students (and players) to teach themselves and others. \nCo-organized with the new Critical Play Lab\, part of UBC’s Pop Culture Cluster; co-sponsored by UBC’s Public Humanities Hub. \n\nTim Bellefleur (he/they) is a Vancouver-based game developer and digital humanist who has spent most of his life playing games and making things. At UBC\, he studied theatre production (BFA 2010)\, Sanskrit theatre (MA 2012)\, and the research and development of technology to assist in the organization\, editing\, analysis\, and presentation of Sanskrit texts\, almost completing a PhD before being lured away from academia into the games industry as a programmer in 2018. He is a lover of experimental games and a strong proponent of collecting as many niche interests and arcane skills as possible. \nChristopher Kawika Guillermo (Dr Christopher B Patterson) is the multi-award-winning author of seven books\, which include the prose-poetry collection Nimrods: a fake-punk self-hurt anti-memoir (Duke University Press\, 2023)\, the essay collection Of Floating Isles: On Growing Pains and Video Games (Arsenal Pulp Press\, 2025)\, and the academic books Open World Empire: Race\, Erotics\, and the Global Rise of Video Games (New York University Press\, 2020)\, and their latest book\, Domesticating Brown: Movements of Racial Imagination (Duke University Press\, 2026). They are the co-editor of two edited collections\, Transpacific\, Undisciplined and Made in Asia/America: Why Video Games Were Never (Really) About Us\, and they were the lead designer of the video game Stamped: an anti-travel game\, an adaptation of their first novel. They are currently an assistant professor at the Institute for Gender\, Race\, Sexuality\, and Social Justice at the University of British Columbia. \nSeries image: detail of Germaine Koh\, Terra.
URL:https://events.ubc.ca/event/pedagogies-of-play/
LOCATION:Green College\, 6201 Cecil Green Park Road\, Vancouver\, British Columbia\, V6T1Z1\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Audience - All,Audience – All Students,Audience – Alumni,Audience – Community,Audience – Faculty and Staff – Vancouver,Audience – Graduate Students,Subject – Entertainment – Media,Subject – Learning And Research – Interdisciplinary,Subject – Learning And Research – Learning Support,Type – Lecture
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://events.ubc.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/UBC-Events-Image_1280x720-32.png
END:VEVENT
