SUMMARY:Presenting Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness in a Dossier
DESCRIPTION:Are you submitting your teaching dossier for tenure\, promotion or reappointment this fall? If so\, join us at this session where we’ll focus on crafting a compelling case for your teaching effectiveness. We’ll look at examples from different disciplines\, and discuss strategies for presenting student feedback\, peer reviews\, and other forms of evidence. You will leave with practical ideas for framing a persuasive account of your teaching impact and momentum to develop your own dossier.​ \nBy the end of this session\, you will be able to:​ \n\nExplain the purpose of the evidence of teaching effectiveness section within a teaching dossier.​\nIdentify a range of possible evidence sources\, including formal student evaluations\, informal feedback\, peer reviews\, and self-reflection.​\nAnalyze dossier examples to explore different ways of presenting evidence.​\nConsider how to frame evidence into a clear\, persuasive narrative about teaching effectiveness.​\nIdentify next steps for developing or refining your own evidence of teaching effectiveness section.\n\nIf you’re unable to make it to this session and are looking for support with your teaching dossier\, see the Teaching Portfolio section of our website (https://ctlt.ubc.ca/resources/teaching/portfolios/) or contact Megan Robertson for a consultation (megan.robertson@ubc.ca) \nPlease register to secure your spot.\nRegister
URL:https://events.ubc.ca/event/presenting-evidence-of-teaching-effectiveness-in-a-dossier-3/
LOCATION:Online/Virtual Event
CATEGORIES:Audience – Faculty and Staff – Okanagan,Audience – Faculty and Staff – Vancouver,Subject – Personal And Professional Development – Faculty
ORGANIZER;CN="Centre for Teaching%2C Learning and Technology":MAILTO:ctlt.info@ubc.ca
END:VEVENT
