SUMMARY:Career Pathways in Engineering and Geoscience: What I Wish I Knew as a Young Professional
DESCRIPTION:Career Pathways in Engineering and Geoscience: What I Wish I Knew as a Young Professional \nHear experienced engineers and geoscientists share the advice they wish they’d had at the start of their careers; from choosing career tracks and pursuing licensure to finding mentors\, advocating for yourself\, and building a values‑aligned career. This panel launches BCSEG’s Emerging Professionals Group\, dedicated to amplifying voices of students\, new graduates\, and early-career professionals across the professions. \nThe panel discussion features six professionals from diverse backgrounds\, fields\, and years of experience. Following the moderated discussion the audience will have time to ask questions to the panel and participate in a networking time for one-on-one connections. \nMeet the Panelists: \nGhazaleh Haghighat\, P.Eng. is a Process Engineer with nearly 10 years of experience in the water and wastewater industry. She has contributed to a wide range of municipal infrastructure projects across Western Canada\, supporting initiatives from early planning and concept development through design and project delivery. \nPrior to joining AECOM\, Ghazaleh worked with Jacobs on major treatment plant upgrade projects throughout Metro Vancouver and previously worked at Metro Vancouver on infrastructure initiatives focused on long-term sustainability. Her experience spans multiple stages of project development and collaboration across multidisciplinary teams. \nTim Dunne\, P.Eng. is a Senior Engineer with over 30 years of experience in civil & structural engineering as a Professional Engineer. Over the years Mr. Dunne’s experience has included structural design\, civil design\, developing products and obtaining patents\, hydro-electric projects\, pedestrian bridges\, pedestrian structures\, industrial facility bridges\, drainage design\, and marina structural design. Mr. Dunne did not start out in Engineering\, he took a long and very important detour thru the Forest Industry as a Forester. Mr. Dunne is married to a Mining Engineer and MBA\, Friday debriefings on the home patio are sometimes heated! \nNima Moazen\, P.Eng.\, SMIEEE is a Hardware Development Engineer at Fortinet Technologies with over 12 years of experience in electrical and hardware engineering. He holds a Master of Applied Science in Electrical Engineering from the University of British Columbia (UBC) and is a licensed Professional Engineer in British Columbia. \nNavpreet Singh Bharaj\, P.Eng\, P.E. is a Principal and Structural Engineer at Ratio Structural Engineering Inc.\, based in the Greater Vancouver area of British Columbia. He holds structural engineering licensure in both Canada and the United States and brings a decade of experience in the design of complex civil\, structural\, and marine infrastructure. Prior to co-leading Ratio Structural Engineering\, Navpreet spent nearly five years as a Structural Engineer with Peter Kiewit Infrastructure Co.\, working on major heavy civil and infrastructure projects across the Pacific Northwest. His experience spans marine and foundation engineering\, seismic design\, and construction-phase structural problem solving on some of the region’s most technically demanding sites\, including the Woodfibre LNG project in Squamish\, British Columbia. Navpreet holds a Master of Engineering in Structural Engineering from the University of Western Ontario\, London\, ON and a Bachelor of Technology in Civil Engineering from Punjabi University in India. \nAmir Taleghani (he/him) is a water resources engineer with over 15 years of work experience primarily in floodplain management and creek engineering. He studied civil engineering at the universities of Waterloo (B.A.Sc.) and British Columbia (M.Eng.). Amir worked at a Burnaby-based consulting engineering firm (Kerr Wood Leidal) for the first 10 years of his career prior to transitioning to local government where he currently works as the senior technical manager for flood and watercourse management at the City of Vancouver. Amir serves as the volunteer chair of Engineers and Geoscientists British Columbia’s Natural Hazards and Natural Resources Advisory Group\, and also volunteers with the Canadian Water Resources Association – BC Chapter when he can. \nNicholas (Nic) Jansen\, PhD\, P.Geo. is a senior geologist at Teck Resources whose career began uniquely as a bush pilot in Northern Canada. He later transitioned into mineral exploration\, focusing primarily on greenfield projects across Southeast Asia\, Australia\, and the Americas. Throughout his professional journey\, Nic has developed deep technical expertise across multiple mineral systems\, including epithermal\, porphyry\, and magmatic sulfide deposits. \nAgenda: \n5:30 pm – Introductions \n5:35 pm – Panel Discussion \n6:45 pm – Live audience questions \n7:00 pm – Networking period \n7:30 pm – Event ends \n  \nCAREER PATHWAYS IN ENGINEERING AND GEOSCIENCE \nPresented by the BC Society of Engineering and Geoscience (BCSEG) and the University of British Columbia (UBC); the “Career Pathways in Engineering and Geoscience” is an in-person free event exclusively offered to UBC Students and Alumni.
URL:https://events.ubc.ca/event/career-pathways-in-engineering-and-geoscience-what-i-wish-i-knew-as-a-young-professional/
LOCATION:HR MacMillan Building\, 2357 Main Mall\, Vancouver\, British Columbia\, V6T1Z4\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Audience – All Students,Audience – Graduate Students,Audience – Undergraduate Students,Subject – Personal And Professional Development – Students,Type – Workshop
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://events.ubc.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/BCSEG-EC-Banner.png
ORGANIZER;CN="UBC Career Centre":MAILTO:career.centre@ubc.ca
END:VEVENT
